<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2025-01-04.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="005934"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#005934" label="[00:59:34]">&lt;plasma41&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: What's slibtool?</span></div><div class="line" id="034125"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#034125" label="[03:41:25]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">clemens3: thank you for that bug report, I'll try to fix what is in savannah and make sure the README has the correct instructions too</span></div><div class="line" id="034851"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#034851" label="[03:48:51]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: keep in mind that I pushed a small commit to github</span></div><div class="line" id="034859"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#034859" label="[03:48:59]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I think just adding --recursive, but pull it first</span></div><div class="line" id="034955"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#034955" label="[03:49:55]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: thank you</span></div><div class="line" id="040015"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#040015" label="[04:00:15]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh and my 2024-2025 git only gpg key has been added to github</span></div><div class="line" id="094927"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#094927" label="[09:49:27]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; Anyone have thoughts on: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/oriansj/mescc-tools-extra/issues/23">https://github.com/oriansj/mescc-tools-extra/issues/23</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="095526"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#095526" label="[09:55:26]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; Makes it not possible to extract live-bootstrap with mescc-tools ungz which currently requires us to use debian midway through our bootstrap process <a rel="nofollow" href="https://codeberg.org/stagex/stagex/src/branch/main/packages/bootstrap/stage1/Containerfile#L3-L18">https://codeberg.org/stagex/stagex/src/branch/main/packages/bootstrap/stage1/Containerfile#L3-L18</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="095557"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#095557" label="[09:55:57]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; or maybe I am a dumb dumb and there is some alternative solution I totally missed, which is entirely possible</span></div><div class="line" id="110002"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#110002" label="[11:00:02]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; Lance Vick: yeah, this is a missing feature...</span></div><div class="line" id="110015"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#110015" label="[11:00:15]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; we have to apply some workarounds after extracting mes too</span></div><div class="line" id="110151"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#110151" label="[11:01:51]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; but required syscall is probably missing from M2libc and kernels too</span></div><div class="line" id="110257"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#110257" label="[11:02:57]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; when does that extraction of yours happen?</span></div><div class="line" id="110304"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#110304" label="[11:03:04]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; still before GNU tar is built?</span></div><div class="line" id="110358"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#110358" label="[11:03:58]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; (oh it must be because this is live-bootstrap source)</span></div><div class="line" id="110958"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#110958" label="[11:09:58]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">plasma41: &quot;slibtool is a libtool implementation written in C&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="111134"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#111134" label="[11:11:34]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's faster, than libtool, i managed to enforce static-linking with it, and it defines another sanity-check for autoreconf</span></div><div class="line" id="111733"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#111733" label="[11:17:33]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i got a suspicion why most distributions do not autoreconf while packaging, and why none would provide static-linking</span></div><div class="line" id="130105"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#130105" label="[13:01:05]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">to my understanding, autotools and [s]libtool were designed to simplify compile-time configuration such as cross-compilation and static-linking</span></div><div class="line" id="130300"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#130300" label="[13:03:00]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's confusing if configure.ac injects hard-coded paths to -I/usr/include and -L/usr/lib when sysroot resides elsewhere, some checks utilize pkg-config, others $PKG_CONFIG, others don't at all, the configure switches vary</span></div><div class="line" id="130534"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#130534" label="[13:05:34]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">up until recently, slibtool picked up some macros from libtool, that i removed now; some packages ship their own m4 macros, others need a specific variant of those for any autoconf version</span></div><div class="line" id="130720"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#130720" label="[13:07:20]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">this significantly complicates re-producibility of builds further</span></div><div class="line" id="131543"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#131543" label="[13:15:43]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">most of related problems are trivial to repair, it is hitting hard however if various cross/compilation variants are involved, for hundreds of builds, with dependencies among them</span></div><div class="line" id="131707"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#131707" label="[13:17:07]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the issue i am having with GNU isn't their license, it's quality in particular with things like autotools, which imply a vendor-lock against GNU</span></div><div class="line" id="131800"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#131800" label="[13:18:00]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and the fact, the time and work i am investing into this, is uncompensated, while their license enforces copy-left without any compensation</span></div><div class="line" id="131949"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#131949" label="[13:19:49]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and it is not possible to distinguish whom work is shared with, with those who play fair, and excluding those who foul play</span></div><div class="line" id="132524"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#132524" label="[13:25:24]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://www.malaymail.com/news/money/2025/01/04/eu-conservative-leader-calls-for-counter-tariffs-on-us-tech-giants-if-trump-imposes-new-trade-barriers/162058">https://www.malaymail.com/news/money/2025/01/04/eu-conservative-leader-calls-for-counter-tariffs-on-us-tech-giants-if-trump-imposes-new-trade-barriers/162058</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="132528"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#132528" label="[13:25:28]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&quot;American digital companies earn a lot of money in the EU and pay hardly any taxes&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="132811"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#132811" label="[13:28:11]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">many bootstrapping issue are caused by anti-competitive habits</span></div><div class="line" id="132953"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#132953" label="[13:29:53]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the Intel roadmap abandoning UEFI/csm is a full scale escalation of trade war</span></div><div class="line" id="133003"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#133003" label="[13:30:03]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the autotools madness is a result of the &quot;unix wars&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="133543"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#133543" label="[13:35:43]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">although i got bundled a complete i486-tcc-linux-musl.iso with development utilities, intel prohibits booting it on many of their laptops without CSM since 2017</span></div><div class="line" id="133715"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#133715" label="[13:37:15]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">although coreboot/seabios exist, such cannot be flashed onto most laptops</span></div><div class="line" id="180834"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#180834" label="[18:08:34]">&lt;plasma41&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: Link to slibtool source?</span></div><div class="line" id="182211"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#182211" label="[18:22:11]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">plasma41: it's easily found online</span></div><div class="line" id="182213"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#182213" label="[18:22:13]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/midipix-project/slibtool">https://github.com/midipix-project/slibtool</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="182305"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#182305" label="[18:23:05]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">integration of it into any packaging system isn't trivial always</span></div><div class="line" id="182333"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#182333" label="[18:23:33]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and for linux-2.x ABI a few simple patches are required</span></div><div class="line" id="184548"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#184548" label="[18:45:48]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; Andrius Štikonas: Can you link to said workaround? Surely it cannot be as bad as mine which is borrowing untar from debian.</span></div><div class="line" id="184605"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#184605" label="[18:46:05]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">sure, it's in live-bootstrap mes script, let me find it</span></div><div class="line" id="184654"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#184654" label="[18:46:54]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/blob/master/steps/mes-0.27/pass1.kaem#L40">https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/blob/master/steps/mes-0.27/pass1.kaem#L40</a>  (--ignore-symlinks)</span></div><div class="line" id="184707"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#184707" label="[18:47:07]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and then manual copies: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/blob/4eceb77e40011b0e4638ca6505fc31a8b0c79273/steps/mes-0.27/pass1.kaem#L50">https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/blob/4eceb77e40011b0e4638ca6505fc31a8b0c79273/steps/mes-0.27/pass1.kaem#L50</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="195246"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#195246" label="[19:52:46]">&lt;gabif&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hi everyone, i've been looking into and tried to use live-bootstrap in the last couple of days</span></div><div class="line" id="195410"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#195410" label="[19:54:10]">&lt;gabif&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so far i learned that my system reboots when the bootstrap process tries to kexec into fiwix</span></div><div class="line" id="195437"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#195437" label="[19:54:37]">&lt;gabif&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(in --bare-metal mode)</span></div><div class="line" id="195958"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#195958" label="[19:59:58]">&lt;gabif&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it worked with --qemu, but i didn't try to do anything further with the result yet</span></div><div class="line" id="200138"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#200138" label="[20:01:38]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">any error?</span></div><div class="line" id="200201"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#200201" label="[20:02:01]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">gabif: also, what kind of system are you using?</span></div><div class="line" id="200212"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#200212" label="[20:02:12]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">BIOS or UEFI with compatibility mode</span></div><div class="line" id="200226"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#200226" label="[20:02:26]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">latter might not work for kernel bootstrapping</span></div><div class="line" id="200340"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#200340" label="[20:03:40]">&lt;gabif&gt;</a></span><span class="message">No, &quot;Starting /boot/fiwix.ext2 at addr ...&quot; and it reboots after in 3-4 seconds</span></div><div class="line" id="200404"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#200404" label="[20:04:04]">&lt;gabif&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, its an UEFI with CSM</span></div><div class="line" id="200821"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#200821" label="[20:08:21]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I think Googulator migth know better but UEFI with CSM often has issues with memory maps</span></div><div class="line" id="200905"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#200905" label="[20:09:05]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so might be &quot;unsupported?</span></div><div class="line" id="201217"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#201217" label="[20:12:17]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for UEFI/amd64 in principle we should write its own &quot;kernel&quot; bootstrap chain</span></div><div class="line" id="201225"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#201225" label="[20:12:25]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and there is some early code but it's not finished</span></div><div class="line" id="201832"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#201832" label="[20:18:32]">&lt;gabif&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i didn't look at what's already done about it</span></div><div class="line" id="202027"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#202027" label="[20:20:27]">&lt;gabif&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'd expect &quot;builder-hex0&quot; stage to be not too hard</span></div><div class="line" id="202406"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#202406" label="[20:24:06]">&lt;gabif&gt;</a></span><span class="message">a stage similar to fiwix could be much more challenging</span></div><div class="line" id="202428"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#202428" label="[20:24:28]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's quite different even in early stage, some stuff is easier, some is harder</span></div><div class="line" id="202452"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#202452" label="[20:24:52]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we basically have stage0-posix, i.e. port of all those early binaries to UEFI</span></div><div class="line" id="202512"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#202512" label="[20:25:12]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">then I started working on some kind of posix kernel wrapper based on builder-hex0 that still runs under UEFI</span></div><div class="line" id="202521"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#202521" label="[20:25:21]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and it can run mes but not any further</span></div><div class="line" id="202554"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#202554" label="[20:25:54]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you are constrained by UEFI until you shut down Boot Services...</span></div><div class="line" id="202608"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#202608" label="[20:26:08]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but once you shut down boot services, you don't have easy I/O</span></div><div class="line" id="202650"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#202650" label="[20:26:50]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(including screen output)</span></div><div class="line" id="202729"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#202729" label="[20:27:29]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so either you accept what UEFI imposes on you (in particular memory management) or you need to write your own code to deal with graphics framebuffer and some FS stuff...</span></div><div class="line" id="202809"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#202809" label="[20:28:09]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">nothing undoable, just didn't have enough people working on it</span></div><div class="line" id="205418"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#205418" label="[20:54:18]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Some UEFIs do put a reserved memory region around the 1GiB mark in CSM mode, which breaks builder-hex0's expectation of a flat memory block between 0x100000 and the start of the MMIO area</span></div><div class="line" id="211719"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#211719" label="[21:17:19]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the tinycc/linux2.4 seems to work without problems atop some uefi/csm on a hp t620 thin client tested with</span></div><div class="line" id="211809"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#211809" label="[21:18:09]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'm still occupied with cleanup tasks to prepare for re-write of packaging compatible with live-bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="212618"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#212618" label="[21:26:18]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">with an old thinkpad t40 e100pro ethernet is detected, ide, sata, smp, for the t620 the gigabit ethernet is not detected</span></div><div class="line" id="212718"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#212718" label="[21:27:18]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'll try to backport a few important drivers to linux-2.4, to keep this broadly compatible</span></div><div class="line" id="212827"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#212827" label="[21:28:27]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">some rtl8188eu[s] wifi usb dongle would be neat, maybe an asix usb-eth dongle, some hardware which is readily available</span></div><div class="line" id="212933"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#212933" label="[21:29:33]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">don't know yet if and how a linux2.4 could best be integrated into kernel-bootstrap seemlessly</span></div><div class="line" id="213034"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#213034" label="[21:30:34]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if tccboot was utilized, this imposed some intermediate limitation (nosmp at the stage linux24 could not be compiled AoT to process related 16bit real-mode asm yet)</span></div><div class="line" id="224849"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#224849" label="[22:48:49]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">urgh, the memory map problem rears it head again</span></div><div class="line" id="224857"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#224857" label="[22:48:57]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and its not going to be easily solvable  lol</span></div><br /></div></body></html>